<h1>Fibonacci tree</h1>
<p>
(data structure)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
A variant of a <a href="binarytree.html"><em>binary tree</em></a> where a tree of order n (n>1) has a left subtree of order n-1 and a right subtree of order n-2.  An order 0 Fibonacci tree has no <a href="node.html"><em>nodes</em></a>, and an order 1 tree has 1 node.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Generalization</strong> (I am a kind of ...)<br>
<a href="binarytree.html"><em>binary tree</em></a>, <a href="avltree.html"><em>AVL tree</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>Aggregate parent</strong> (I am a part of or used in ...)<br>
<a href="fibonaccianSearch.html"><em>Fibonaccian search</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>Aggregate child</strong> (... is a part of or used in me.)<br>
<a href="fibonacciNumber.html"><em>Fibonacci number</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
A Fibonacci tree of order n has F(n+2)-1 nodes, where F(n) is the n<sup>th</sup> <a href="fibonacciNumber.html"><em>Fibonacci number</em></a>.  A Fibonacci tree is the most unbalanced <a href="avltree.html"><em>AVL tree</em></a> allowed.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>
<h2>More information</h2>
